{
  "title": "Γεια σου!",
  "translation": "Hi!",
  "topics": "The verb *είμαι* (to be), the personal pronouns, the article before names, *δεν* and questions, *εσύ* or *εσείς*; greetings, please and thank you, what to say when you don't understand",
  "interface": "english",
  "keys": "αβγδεζηθικλμνξοπρστςυφχψωάέήίόύώ",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "In this lesson you learn the first building blocks of Greek grammar: the verb *είμαι* (to be), the personal pronouns, the article in front of names, how to say *not* and ask a question, and when to use the polite *εσείς*. After the grammar you'll find the everyday phrases that go with it: greetings, *please* and *thank you*, and what to say when you don't understand.",
      {
        "grid": [
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "The verb *είμαι* (to be)",
              "head": [
                "",
                "είμαι",
                "example"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "εγώ",
                  "*είμαι*",
                  "*Είμαι η Άννα.*"
                ],
                [
                  "εσύ",
                  "*είσαι*",
                  "*Είσαι καλά;*"
                ],
                [
                  "αυτός, αυτή, αυτό",
                  "*είναι*",
                  "*Αυτή είναι η Μαρία.*"
                ],
                [
                  "εμείς",
                  "*είμαστε*",
                  "*Είμαστε φίλοι.*"
                ],
                [
                  "εσείς",
                  "*είστε* (*είσαστε*)",
                  "*Πώς είστε;*"
                ],
                [
                  "αυτοί, αυτές, αυτά",
                  "*είναι*",
                  "*Είναι εδώ.*"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "*Είναι* means both *he/she/it is* and *they are*. The ending tells you who it is, so the pronoun is usually left out: *Είμαι καλά* (I'm fine), *Είσαι εδώ;* (Are you here?).",
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "Personal pronouns",
              "head": [
                "",
                "singular",
                "plural"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "1st",
                  "*εγώ* (I)",
                  "*εμείς* (we)"
                ],
                [
                  "2nd",
                  "*εσύ* (you)",
                  "*εσείς* (you: several people, or one person politely)"
                ],
                [
                  "3rd",
                  "*αυτός, αυτή, αυτό* (he, she, it)",
                  "*αυτοί, αυτές, αυτά* (they)"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "Use a pronoun only to stress or contrast: *Εγώ είμαι η Άννα. Εσύ;* (I'm Anna. And you?) *Αυτός* is *he*, *αυτή* is *she*; for *they*, *αυτοί* is for men or a mixed group, *αυτές* for women only, *αυτά* for things.",
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "wide": true,
            "table": {
              "title": "The article before a name",
              "head": [
                "",
                "example",
                "note"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "a man: *ο*",
                  "*Είμαι ο Νίκος.* *Αυτός είναι ο κύριος Γιώργος.*",
                  "*ο* = the (masculine)"
                ],
                [
                  "a woman: *η*",
                  "*Είμαι η Άννα.* *Αυτή είναι η κυρία Ελένη.*",
                  "*η* = the (feminine)"
                ],
                [
                  "speaking to someone",
                  "*Γεια σου, Νίκο!* *Καλημέρα, κύριε Γιώργο!*",
                  "no article, and many men's names lose the final *-ς*"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "In Greek a name usually has the article in front of it, as in *ο Νίκος, η Άννα*: without it the sentence sounds incomplete. When you speak *to* someone, the article goes and men's names in *-ος* change their ending: *Νίκος → Νίκο*, *κύριος → κύριε*. Women's names don't change: *Γεια σου, Άννα!*"
          },
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "No and questions",
              "head": [
                "form",
                "example",
                "note"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "*δεν* + verb",
                  "*Δεν είμαι καλά.*",
                  "*not*: always right before the verb"
                ],
                [
                  "*όχι*",
                  "— *Είσαι η Άννα;* — *Όχι.*",
                  "*no*, as an answer on its own"
                ],
                [
                  "*ναι*",
                  "— *Είσαι ο Νίκος;* — *Ναι.*",
                  "*yes*: it sounds like English *ne*, not *nay*"
                ],
                [
                  "question",
                  "*Είσαι καλά;*",
                  "same words as the statement; the voice goes up, and the question mark is ;"
                ],
                [
                  "question word",
                  "*Πώς είσαι;* *Τι κάνεις;*",
                  "the question word comes first"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "With a verb, *not* is always *δεν*, right before it: ✗ *Όχι είμαι καλά*. *Όχι* answers a question on its own or starts the answer: *Όχι, δεν είμαι η Άννα.* A question has the same words and word order as a statement: only the voice (going up) and the Greek question mark (;) change.",
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "wide": true,
            "table": {
              "title": "*Εσύ* or *εσείς*?",
              "head": [
                "",
                "to a friend (*εσύ*)",
                "politely (*εσείς*)"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "hello",
                  "*Γεια σου!*",
                  "*Γεια σας!*"
                ],
                [
                  "how are you?",
                  "*Τι κάνεις;* *Πώς είσαι;*",
                  "*Τι κάνετε;* *Πώς είστε;*"
                ],
                [
                  "are you…?",
                  "*Είσαι ο Νίκος;*",
                  "*Είστε ο κύριος Παπαδόπουλος;*"
                ],
                [
                  "and you?",
                  "*Εσύ;*",
                  "*Εσείς;*"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "Greek has no special polite pronoun: to be polite to one person, you use the plural *εσείς* and the plural verb, as in French *vous*. Use it with people you don't know, older people, in shops and offices. Friends, family, children and people your age get *εσύ*."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "Phrases for every day"
      },
      "Learn these as fixed phrases: you'll use them in every conversation.",
      {
        "grid": [
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "Hello and goodbye",
              "head": [
                "Greek",
                "meaning",
                "when"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "*Καλημέρα!*",
                  "good morning",
                  "when you meet someone, until about midday"
                ],
                [
                  "*Καλησπέρα!*",
                  "good evening",
                  "when you meet someone, from late afternoon"
                ],
                [
                  "*Καληνύχτα!*",
                  "good night",
                  "when you leave in the evening or go to bed"
                ],
                [
                  "*Γεια σου!*",
                  "hi, bye",
                  "to a friend, a child, someone your age"
                ],
                [
                  "*Γεια σας!*",
                  "hello, goodbye",
                  "politely, or to several people"
                ],
                [
                  "*Χαίρετε!*",
                  "hello, goodbye",
                  "formal, in shops and offices"
                ],
                [
                  "*Τα λέμε!*",
                  "see you",
                  "informal, when you leave"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "*Γεια* means *health*: *Γεια σου* is *health to you*, and it works both when you arrive and when you leave. *Αντίο* also means *goodbye*, but it sounds final: Greeks prefer *Γεια σου* or *Τα λέμε*.",
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "How are you?",
              "head": [
                "Greek",
                "meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "*Τι κάνεις;* / *Τι κάνετε;*",
                  "How are you? (to a friend / polite)"
                ],
                [
                  "*Πώς είσαι;* / *Πώς είστε;*",
                  "How are you? (to a friend / polite)"
                ],
                [
                  "*Πολύ καλά!*",
                  "very well"
                ],
                [
                  "*Καλά, ευχαριστώ. Εσύ;*",
                  "fine, thanks. And you?"
                ],
                [
                  "*Έτσι κι έτσι.*",
                  "so-so"
                ],
                [
                  "*Χάλια!*",
                  "awful! (informal)"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "*Τι κάνεις;* literally means *What are you doing?*, but as a greeting it is just *How are you?*"
          },
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "Being polite",
              "head": [
                "Greek",
                "meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "*Παρακαλώ.*",
                  "please; you're welcome"
                ],
                [
                  "*Ευχαριστώ (πολύ).*",
                  "thank you (very much)"
                ],
                [
                  "*Τίποτα.*",
                  "don't mention it (literally: nothing)"
                ],
                [
                  "*Συγγνώμη.*",
                  "sorry; excuse me"
                ],
                [
                  "*Με συγχωρείτε.*",
                  "excuse me (polite)"
                ],
                [
                  "*Ορίστε.*",
                  "here you are; pardon?"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "The answer to *Ευχαριστώ* is *Παρακαλώ* or *Τίποτα*. In a shop, *Παρακαλώ;* also means *Can I help you?*"
          },
          {
            "table": {
              "title": "When you don't understand",
              "head": [
                "Greek",
                "meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "*Δεν καταλαβαίνω.*",
                  "I don't understand."
                ],
                [
                  "*Μιλάτε αγγλικά;*",
                  "Do you speak English?"
                ],
                [
                  "*Πιο αργά, παρακαλώ.*",
                  "Slower, please."
                ],
                [
                  "*Ξανά, παρακαλώ.*",
                  "Again, please."
                ],
                [
                  "*Τι σημαίνει «…»;*",
                  "What does … mean?"
                ],
                [
                  "*Πώς λέγεται … στα ελληνικά;*",
                  "How do you say … in Greek?"
                ]
              ]
            },
            "note": "These six sentences are your lifeline: with them you can keep any conversation going.",
            "wide": true
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
